Dragon Burst 0188: a host GPU loss no longer aborts the process

GraphicsSystem::OnHostGpuLossFromAnyThread called rex::FatalError (std::abort). With a handler set by the
app (SetHostGpuLossHandler) the command processor just stops submitting (device_lost_, as before), the
loss is logged and flushed and the app takes over (Android: back to the launcher with a notice).
Without a handler nothing changes. gpu_test_loss_s = N simulates a loss N s after the guest GPU starts.

diff --git a/include/rex/graphics/graphics_system.h b/include/rex/graphics/graphics_system.h
index 85d72189744039aace5b942dbb9867ffbb48a3c9..c1bd26531e350bd7b0f9ecf2fa52b707c868d8c8 100644
--- a/include/rex/graphics/graphics_system.h
+++ b/include/rex/graphics/graphics_system.h
@@ -46,6 +46,14 @@ namespace rex::graphics {
 
 class CommandProcessor;
 
+// Dragon Burst 0188: called once, from any thread, when the host GPU device is lost
+// (VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST). With a handler set the command processor just stops submitting
+// and the app decides what to do (Android: back to the launcher with a notice) instead of
+// the process aborting. reason: a short description for the user and the log.
+using HostGpuLossHandler = void (*)(const char* reason);
+// Exported: the graphics library builds with hidden visibility (see 0108) and the app calls it.
+__attribute__((visibility("default"))) void SetHostGpuLossHandler(HostGpuLossHandler handler);
+
 class GraphicsSystem : public system::IGraphicsSystem {
  public:
   virtual ~GraphicsSystem();
